NAME
    abi - named BIOS jump-table and OS syscall addresses (//#use abi, ;#use abi)

SYNOPSIS
    //#use abi          C:   cc splices /lib/lib_abi.c
    ;#use abi           asm: mkasm.sh appends /lib/lib_abi.inc

DESCRIPTION
    Object-like #defines that NAME the BIOS jump table ($01xx) and the OS
    syscall vector ($20xx), so a command writes

        bios(FOPEN, RDBUF, 0)        instead of  bios(0x0124, 0xFC00, 0)

    #define is a compile-time TEXTUAL substitution, so bios()'s "address
    must be a literal" requirement is still met -- and, unlike a wrapper
    function, it costs ZERO code space. Each address lives here alone, so
    the firmware can renumber its jump table / syscall vector by editing
    this file (and the asm twin lib_abi.inc) alone. The asm equates emit
    NO bytes, so switching raw `JSR $NNNN` calls to symbolic ones
    assembles byte-identically.

RETURN CONVENTION
    bios() returns the routine's A in the low byte with the carry flag in
    bit 8, so callers keep their `& 256` tests:

        if (bios(FOPEN, RDBUF, 0) & 256) ...        /* carry = not found */
        c = bios(FGETB, 0, 0);
        while ((c & 256) == 0) { ... c = bios(FGETB, 0, 0); }  /* carry = EOF */

    Commands should name every BIOS/OS entry through this library rather
    than hardcode a vector -- see the "named BIOS/OS addresses" note in
    the programmer docs. Scratch/graphics/TPA addresses come from a
    separate generated library; see man mem.

SEE ALSO
    mem, apath, dirent, err
